evidence.statement:
The five hospitals experienced:
- occupancy rates of approximately 87–100%;
- recurring pressure from scheduled surgeries;
- increased emergency-department admissions;
- emergency-department boarding;
- system-wide capacity strain.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748

The 2026 study found that hospitals continuously make operational decisions across:
- entry;
- internal flow;
- transfer;
- discharge.
It also found that hospitals use both:
- decentralized local decision making;
- centralized coordination.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748

Three of the five hospitals had established physical command centres as coordination hubs.
These centres consolidated previously decentralized resources and aimed to provide:
- hospital-wide oversight;
- faster communication;
- coordinated capacity management;
- more efficient patient-flow decisions.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748

The study identifies centrally coordinated detailed reprioritization, including situations where hospitals:
- intervene in stalled patient progressions;
- reallocate ICU capacity;
- reallocate OR capacity for urgent admissions.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748

ServiceNow currently provides Healthcare Operations applications intended to streamline and automate healthcare operational tasks.
The Healthcare Operations portfolio includes:
- Healthcare Operations Core;
- Care Team Operations for Healthcare IT;
- Care Team Operations for Biomed;
- Care Team Operations for Environmental Services;
- Care Team Operations for Facilities.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/healthcare-life-sciences/healthcare-operations-overview.html

ServiceNow Healthcare and Life Sciences Service Management provides:
- patient and care-team workflows;
- playbooks;
- case management;
- healthcare workspace;
- integration with external healthcare systems.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/healthcare-life-sciences/healthcare-and-life-sciences-service-management/hcls-service-mgmt-overview.html

Healthcare Workspace allows healthcare agents to:
- accept healthcare requests;
- access patient information;
- manage healthcare-related cases;
- associate patient information with interactions.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/healthcare-life-sciences/healthcare-and-life-sciences-service-management-core/hcls-using-workspace.html

ServiceNow Workforce Optimization supports scheduling and resource management.
For ITSM, ServiceNow documents assigning work based on:
- skills;
- availability;
- capacity;
- shifts.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/it-service-management/workforce-optimization-for-it-service-management/track-tasks-teams-wfo-itsm.html

ServiceNow's Schedule Optimization engine:
- automates task assignment;
- creates efficient schedules;
- adapts to changing conditions;
- uses policies;
- uses constraints;
- supports objectives such as minimizing travel;
- supports skill requirements.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/field-service-scheduling/schedule-optimization-engine.html

The current ServiceNow evidence does not establish native capability for:
- hospital-wide bed-flow optimization;
- patient acuity-aware bed allocation;
- clinical priority balancing;
- ICU/OR/ward dependency modeling;
- discharge-readiness propagation;
- ED boarding optimization;
- system-wide patient-flow scenario comparison;
- hospital command-centre decision support.

The documented state contains:
- an anticipated discharge date of the current day;
- an outstanding laboratory dependency;
- a resulting barrier to progression;
- command-centre visibility of the barrier.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748

A centralized patient-flow management centre at an academic health system integrated:
- bed management;
- case management;
- environmental services;
- patient transport;
- transfer operations.
The centre used a single technology platform and round-the-clock patient placement, with daily bed meetings driving action plans.
Reported measures included:
- ED walkouts;
- ED boarding;
- ambulance diversion;
- transfer volume;
- lost transfers;
- time to bed assignment;
- bed turnover time.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1177/1062860614564618

A case study of bed management during COVID-19 documented:
- unexpected demand growth;
- bed scarcity;
- recruitment of approximately 500 affiliated private-sector beds;
- bed conversion and reconversion;
- internal patient logistics;
- capacity creation according to changing demand.
Source:
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10298121/

Human judgment is explicitly present.
The command centre:
- identifies the patient;
- identifies the barrier;
- determines the barrier requires intervention;
- coordinates with another department;
- facilitates prioritization.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748

The 2026 study reports that some hospitals use:
- EHR-integrated command centres;
- tools highlighting patients close to discharge;
- EHR-integrated chat;
- capacity-management tools;
- tools identifying delayed tasks.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748

A UK NHS command-centre study found that a centralized command centre consolidated real-time information covering:
- hospital capacity;
- emergency-department status;
- patient transfers;
- discharge tasks;
- care progression;
- patient deterioration.
Source:
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10566538/

A UK NHS command-centre implementation study describes digital tiles showing:
- confirmed discharges;
- capacity snapshots;
- transfers;
- ED status.
Staff then manually compiled a situational report and updated it as information changed.
Source:
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608708/

The 2026 multi-hospital patient-flow study reports a Hospital U example in which:
- a patient was expected to discharge that day;
- a laboratory task was outstanding;
- the command-centre manager could identify the patient and barrier;
- the laboratory was coordinated to prioritize the patient.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748

The documented ServiceNow capability is directly relevant to:
- identifying an operational issue;
- routing it to a supporting department;
- tracking fulfillment;
- coordinating care-team operational work.

A 2026 multiple-case study of five leading academic hospitals, based on 157 interviews with 112 informants, documents hospital-wide patient-flow decision making.
The study reports that hospitals combine:
- predicted admissions;
- anticipated discharges;
- staffing availability;
to assess daily capacity and determine whether outflow can safely be increased.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748
The same study documents:
- centralized command centres;
- EHR integration;
- discharge bottleneck identification;
- laboratory prioritization;
- clinical expeditors;
- ICU prioritization;
- bed management;
- internal transfers;
- OR scheduling;
- capacity coordination.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748

Bradford Royal Infirmary's command centre used software and display screens providing real-time information on:
- overall hospital capacity;
- emergency-department status;
- patient transfers;
- discharge tasks;
- care progression;
- patient deterioration.
Source:
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10566538/

AI can improve:
- intake;
- triage;
- case creation;
- routing;
- operational handoff.
### But:
No cited documentation establishes that these healthcare agents natively solve the multi-objective hospital-flow prioritization problem in this scenario.

Typical decisions include:
- triage;
- bedside prioritization;
- discharge authorization;
- patient-level progression.
These decisions require:
- clinical judgment;
- immediacy;
- contextual knowledge;
- patient-specific information.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748
### Decision logic
INDIVIDUAL PATIENT
+
IMMEDIATE CONTEXT
+
CLINICAL / OPERATIONAL KNOWLEDGE
↓
LOCAL DECISION

This includes ward-level adjustments such as:
- reorganizing tasks;
- adjusting workloads;
- advancing clusters of discharge-ready patients;
- buffering moment-to-moment variability.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748
### Decision logic
MULTIPLE PATIENTS
+
LOCAL WORKLOAD
+
LOCAL VARIABILITY
↓
WARD-LEVEL REBALANCING
### Criteria indicated by the evidence
- workload balance;
- local capacity;
- immediate variability;
- contextual knowledge;
- responsiveness.

The study describes relational governance:
- credibility;
- trust;
- responsiveness;
- professional expertise.
Central managers act more as facilitators than command-and-control authorities.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748

