However:
> The study does not measure how many minutes per decision are spent synthesizing information.

The opportunity cannot simply be:
> "Hospitals need a better AI decision engine."
That is too broad.
Existing hospitals already use:
- command centres;
- predictive tools;
- scheduling tools;
- algorithms;
- EHR integration;
- human clinical/operational judgment.

The command-centre design included integrated views such as:
- emergency-department status;
- inbound patients awaiting bed assignment and transport;
- bed availability by functional unit;
- forecast occupancy;
- other patient-flow and capacity feeds.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcjq.2018.11.006
The design explicitly transformed disparate system feeds into a consolidated operational view.

The 2026 multiple-case study of five leading academic hospitals describes command centres integrating:
- real-time data;
- predictive analytics;
- decision-support systems;
- capacity information;
- patient-flow information;
- resource-allocation information.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748
The study explicitly describes command centres as centralized units integrating real-time data and predictive analytics to manage capacity, patient flow and resource allocation.

A UK NHS command-centre study describes the Bradford command centre as providing real-time information on:
- overall hospital capacity;
- emergency-department status;
- patient transfers;
- discharge tasks;
- care progression;
- patient deterioration.
Source:
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10566538/
The system drew information from electronic health records and other health information systems into a consolidated command-centre environment.
Source:
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10566538/

A 2026 multi-hospital study reports that some hospitals use:
- predictive analytics;
- AI/ML;
- algorithms predicting bed availability;
- specialized surgical scheduling tools;
- EHR-integrated decision support;
- command-centre dashboards.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748
Therefore:
> **Cross-system synthesis cannot simply be equated with an absence of technology.**

The evidence identifies several roles:
- command-centre directors;
- command-centre managers;
- clinical expeditors;
- bed managers;
- occupancy managers;
- physicians;
- nurses;
- operational managers.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748
The NHS AI-command-centre evaluation additionally describes staff triangulating and verifying information from multiple sources.
Source:
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608696/

The NHS mixed-methods evaluation found:
- data-quality concerns;
- outdated or incomplete records;
- discrepancies between systems and frontline reports;
- need for triangulation and verification.
Source:
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608696/
The 2019 command-centre design found significant effort and time were required to access information across seven poorly interfaced systems before integration.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcjq.2018.11.006

The NHS artificial-intelligence command-centre evaluation provides direct evidence that data quality remained a practical operational issue.
The study reports that:
- electronic records were not always sufficiently up to date, accurate, or complete;
- staff often had to triangulate and verify information from multiple sources and systems;
- discrepancies existed between system data and what frontline staff reported;
- compromise, goodwill, and a shared sense of purpose were necessary for the command centre to function effectively.
Source:
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608696/
Supporting ethnographic findings:
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608708/

inferences.statement:
A ServiceNow implementation could plausibly:
1. receive or create an operational case;
2. associate the task with the responsible supporting department;
3. prioritize the task;
4. route/escalate it;
5. coordinate fulfillment;
6. allow human review;
7. record completion.
Healthcare Operations Core directly supports the case/fulfillment pattern.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/healthcare-life-sciences/healthcare-operations-core/hcls-cto-exploring.html

But configuration alone would still require:
- a data model;
- decision rules;
- prioritization logic;
- constraints;
- escalation logic;
- integration;
- validation.
No evidence currently demonstrates that ServiceNow ships a ready-made patient-flow optimizer for this exact problem.

hypotheses.statement:
There is unusually strong evidence that:
- hospitals combine predicted admissions, anticipated discharges, and staffing availability;
- centralized command centres coordinate capacity;
- clinical expeditors identify delayed tasks;
- patient-flow systems consolidate multiple operational signals;
- human teams intervene across departmental boundaries.
Source:
https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/09537287.2026.2655748
This is enough to justify investigating the **decision-synthesis burden**.
It is not enough to justify building a solution.

This is narrower than:
- workflow automation;
- capacity management;
- patient-flow dashboards;
- predictive analytics;
- resource scheduling.

